


				COURTYARDS

ÇEKIRDEKSIZ - They also make “somun” bread in summer, like at KH. Summer food preparation for winter  consists of “tarhana”, “kuskus” which is made of bulgur rolled in egg, flour and some milk...it becomes little balls of wheat, which is then cooked like rice, with addition of fresh tomatoes and peppers. It is quite delicious.KIRANHARMAN -The house is approached through a long garden, within a courtyard, at the end of which is the house...in the center is the raised “hayat” or “seki” and on each side of it are the residences of the son and his family and the old lady with her two grown, unmarried sons. The interior is absolutely spotless, freshly painted, low divans with kilims, embroidered sheets over the back pillows....However, the da-in-law’s house has all the carved, shiny urban furniture.....There is an oven to one side of the garden courtyard, which was built by Safiye. It is the same type as YH ovens, they bake  “somun” loaves in trays which are set on metal tripods in the oven....white flour mixed with whole wheat and very well kneaded.  Allowed to raise several times..the result is a very spongy, raised bread...Advantage of this bread is that it keeps longer than “bazlama” which lasts a day or two. YH people say that all “göcmens” make good somun. (They make bazlama in winter). The courtyard consists of a long mudbrick building, with pitch tile roof-- This building is divided up by interior walls and doors from exterior, into several storage areas: hay, old plough, remnants of carriage with wooden wheels, metal and wooden tools (pitchfork (dirgen), capa,  döven, old machinery for seeding etc..) sheepfold, cow pen. Other individual buildings include an outhouse, chicken coop, tandır house which they call “mutfak”. Actual tandır is not as deep and wide as at YH. (It is not used as much). It also doubles for storage of pickled vegetables and fruits, jams etc...For example, Safiye was pickling plums for winter..she makes Xs with a knife, sundries them...when they want it, they will make it into compote by boiling it with sugar water...In the meantime, it is kept in a sack after drying...	There are two interesting storage areas which I have not seen at YH.(1) wheat storage, an elevated space, ca. 5x5 m. area, built of mudbrick and carefully plastered over. The space is divided into 3,4 compartments, by .50 m.high partitions. And, instead of being on the floor, the wheat is elevated so as to protect it from water, dust and dirt. Safiye told me the partitions separated the different kinds of wheat, which they used to use in the past, for making different foods--- Asure wheat, macaroni wheat, bulgur wheat, breadwheat...(2) Part of the hay barn has 2 rectangular pits, each ca, 1x.50 m., and .50 m. deep, plastered inside roughly. These are used for cold storage of cheese, potatoes and onions.Cheese is kept in plastic jars with lid now (used to be ceramic), in brine...the pits are covered with earth through summer and fall until winter...Both of these storage areas, partitioned and elevated wheat storage and rectangular pit dug into the floor of part of a hay storage barn would leave archaeological traces, and therefore could be used in the interpretation of the data from the ancient remains. Furthermore, ethnographically interesting is the fact that these people are ethnically different from pop. of YH.The plastering on the walls have a fan-shaped pattern; it is put on by hand, she said.“Somun” Making -	It is made by the mother every 2-3 days in the summer. Each time she makes 6-7 loaves for a family of 5 adults, 2 young kids. Recipe is:1) Mix ca. 10 kg. of flour with handful of yeast, salt and enough water to make it into a soft dough. (She also adds some leftover dough with yeast, from previous time).. 2) Knead it for ca. 45 minutes vigorously, leave it to rise twice at room temp. near a sunny window. It rises in 2 hours, she kneads it again---after 1/2 hr. pours it into greased pans3) let rise in the pans-- in the meantime make fire in fırın in the front of “hayat” in courtyard..4) Oven is lit with stalk and a little hay--it takes about 20-25 minutes to get hot.5) Put pans of bread on tripods, use a long-handled metal fork to place them into the oven.  (The interior of oven is big enough for 7 pans). Close the opening with a sheet metal which fits perfectly over the opening.In 45 minutes the bread is baked to a golden crisp. (She did not even check it before 45 minutes--she has the whole timing down pat).IT IS A WONDERFUL, SPONGY BREAD!!The oven is made by Safiye, plastered over every year by her.Daily Cycle - Breakfast consists of soup, fried chicken, cheese, olives and tea...very different from tea-cheese-butter-jam combination of typical Turkish breakfast.While the grandmother is in charge of washing dishes, cooking, baking and making preserves, the da-in-law is cleaning the house, tending her children and the laundry. Children are taken care of by whoever is around...flexible and easy going. Building Activities - Early September is the month when all repairing and building activities take place. Roofs are mended, mudbrick is made to repair walls, build new outhouses, animal pens which are leaking are repaired--this is done mainly by men, fathers and sons. Women do plastering of ovens in courtyard and tandır house, parts of house walls, also at this time. SABANÖZÜ -They have “tandır” houses, as others, but they do not have ovens in the courtyard..or anywhere else. ..that is interesting. 	Fall Activities in CY:  One of the activities is pickling vegetables.. and making vinegar from grapes and small pears (ahlat). Salted water is added to grapes/ahlat, kept in plastic jars with lids. Stayes in the sun for ca. two months, and it gets sour..and added to the different vegetables-- tomatoes, cucumbers, lahana, carrots, aubergines, cauliflower....topped with parsley; lots of garlic added. Reason for Sept. is that this is the month for availability of all kinds of vegetables.Bagging of onions, after cutting stem and separating it into different sizesMAKARNA KESMEKSALÇACourtyard Activities in summer months:	1) ekmek	2) sun dry apricots and other fruits	3) jams in large cauldrons, made on hearth	4) tarhana, eriˆte	5) bulghur boiled in large cauldrons	6) shearing, washing and cleaning wool	7) gardening (weeding, watering)	8) repairing plaster and whitewashing outer walls	9) Daily Herding Activities in CY:	1) milking; 2) milk processing; 3) preparing food for father/shepherd;	4) “agıl” cleaning  ; 5) Processing of gübreGübre processing is an important part of the winter preparation, both for the fields and for fuel...It is rather detailed, painstaking work which requires good timing..	- Fertilizer comes from the droppings of sheep and goats, piled on the summer agıl which is open...it is used in smaller amounts than chemical fertilizer, but is said to be stronger, especially used on wheat fields...Farmers without herds buy it from others in the village...	-“Mayıs” is cow shit which has been made into fuel...combined with water and little bit of straw....then, put into molds, so that it is a circular cake, 6-7 cm. thickness. It is dried on both sides, set against a wall or piled up like a pyramid, each row slightly staggered so that plenty of air passes through it..It is not as strong and long-lasting fuel as “kerme” (sheep and goat shit).It is used mainly for heating tandır, hearth/oven..Some poor and elderly women who make these cakes are allowed to keep it for fuel.	- “Kerme” is shoveled out twice a year from underneath the sheep, as part of their debris- its thickness is ca. 5-10 cm. and it is piled up in chunks on one side of the courtyard to dry..it is shapeless.. mainly used for heating stove, also used for tandır and hearth/oven, for breadmaking and general baking.Both gübre and cow shit are collected in or outside of CY to dry...before taking it to the fields and making it into dung cakes.....BULGUR - MAKING in CY (Pembe Kayran)	August-Sept. activity	26.7.96Ibrahim Kayran knows just as much about bulghur making as his wife Pembe. But, of course, he will not do it. It is considered to be ‘woman’s job’.He learned it from watching his mother and grandmother. So did Pembe, after she got married...(It is very interesting that there is no stigma attached to young boys doing girls’ tasks, until they go to military service, or 18-19...then, they are considered men. For ex. Emrah, a boy of 13, came to a neighbor’s courtyard with his mother, sat down and started crocheting a kitchen towel...I thought may be he was a girl....none of the men or women there thought it was strange...he had learned it from his grandmother, while living with her at Polatlı...So, gender separation is not intentionally done at a young age, as part of children’s early training)!Two large cauldrons of copper in which asure bugday are boiling...each contains 20 kg. of wheat...These are traditional pots for festive food, like wedding pilav, asure etc...While it boils, it swells and gets soft, almost eatable stage...Since they are very large cauldrons, Pembe made a quick hearth with 6 mudbricks, placed two tripods in each on which the cauldrons sit. They are set close to the kitchen, in the courtyard so that she can frequently pour water into it as the bulghur absorbs it while cooking, and turn it about with “delikli kepçe”...It requires constant attention, as lack of enough water will cause the bottom layers to burn. Boiling in its water allows the nutrients from the husk to be absorbed...so, more healthy. Takes ca. 1-2 hours to boil.I make it for dolma, (finer version), pilav, kısır, çorba etc... After boiling it is laid on heavy cotton cloths to dry in the sun; while it dries it shrinks and wrinkles...it is turned around many times while drying...Then it is stored in sacks until it is ground up either by hand-mill or at the commercial mill in Polatlı. (Safure had shown me how she does it with a hand mill which she borrowed from Fatma nine--I have a slide of it--Milling it separates the husk from the kernel; after milling we separate it into 3 sizes in the collander ...for different foods.Only women make it; men bring the wheat, take bulgur for milling..I also make it to give as presents to my children..it is clean, home-made and more nutritious than the commercial one.At home we can make a lot, it is economical, it is also very clean.Ibrahim ‘s comments on Bulghur -In the past when we made it in the courtyard, neighbors used to drop by and taste it. Few people do it now. Shows relationship of socialization of traditional food-processing activities. After bulghur has been dried, it was milled at home (dibek) or “ding” which is made of a millstone; it is placed in a circular thing..Young men and women used to congregate at each other’s home (courtyard) and do it with dibek. They enjoyed doing it, sing along and eat some foods. Adults sieved it into different sizes. I never did it but I saw my mother do it, and helped her.Bulghhur, after drying can stay in a bag, without humidity, for several years.What is its nutrition, over and above wheat? Its husk contains as much protein as wheat...but it has no calories. But it is hard to digest.More nutritious than rice. Etli, nohutlu, mercimekli bulgur...Içine kavurma da konur. Yine kusbası ile de yapılır. Kabartma pilav, veya sulu pilav.My sisters live in Sivrihisar. They are both older, widows, they have grandchildren.NISASTA YAPILISI - (Pembe) Made in hot weather.It is the essence of wheat. WE  SIEVE, AND WASH the wheat. Put it in large cauldron with water, for about a week...it splits, milky essence (nisasta) comes out. Wash the wheat again, put it in collander, then we grind it in meat grinder...put it back in the water, husk goes on top, nisasta settles in bottom, like yoghurt. We take off the juice from above it, take off the husk..we sift it several times so it becomes sweet...with a cloth filled with clean earth, we put the nisasta in it slowly. Earth completely oozes out the water from nisasta, becomes like cheese; we sieve it through several sized sieves.....then, we dry it in the sun. We make muhallebi with it. We  mix it with sugar and water, boil it a bit, then put melted butter over it. Very good in winter, it is called PALIZE. (have good pictures)Dogan CY - 						26/9/95They practice a good combination of agricult and herding -  mixed farming.For 1 week, there is a flurry of activities involving onion cutting of stalks, separating into sizes, drying and bagging. Drawing the courtyard with Onur. Finding out the functions of all installations. They have about 120 sheep, 15-18 cows. Three brothers share the courtyard. Behind houses are sheepfolds, storage places, open “agıl” where sheep sit in summer...A separate place for sheep without lambs...The droppings from the open “agıl” is piled up to one corner of the CY, then, it is taken to the fields as fertilizer. In addition, it has another function in the winter, as ground-cover:  first it is kept in a dry storage space, then laid on the floor of the space where the sheep sit outdoors to eat and indoors where the lambs sit. Kerme comes out in large pieces, dried and placed in the dung storage, used as fuel for winter.In this CY, in the sub-basement of Fatma’s (deaf-dumb) house, there is a pantry (kiler).. Also Karagöz family has it...contains all kinds of pickled vegetables, from mushroom to cabbage, carrot...butter, cheese, tomato paste, dry beans...Storage areas - have animal feed (15-16 ton of barley for a year)....it is kept separately from wheat storage.. it is the same everywhere. In addition, there is storage for fertilizer, tezek, equipment for ploughing and other parts, hay machine, patos...In another corner of the CY is a pile of the sugar beet pulp (küspe)...this is also kept as animal feed.Among other piles in CY is an ash pile from the hearth which is also mixed with fertilizer. In front of sheep pens there are wooden troughs for feed and water..these are long and narrow,  dugout from a tree- they are portable so that they can be carried outside and inside-Ibrahim Kayran’s wheat storage at CY -  6/8/96We are drawing  it with Alan. There are 3 divisions, by semi-permanent walls of ca. 50 cm. separating 2 kinds of wheat and cow feed. The utensils are on the floor or hanging from the wall: - wooden trough with hay and animal feed (tekne where bread is also made) - shovel, kürek - pair of mill stones, el degirmen tası - several sized sieves, elek - wooden barrels, fıçı,  in which is stored barley for duck feed - woven sacks in which there is cow feed, clover (yonca) & vetch (burçak) - common broom	␀		␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀	␀	␀␀␀␀	␀	␀␀␀	
